Send literal data in chunks of up to 8 MiB instead of 32 KiB

For rsync 3.5.2. Apply it with "patch -p1" in the rsync source directory.

rsync sends the file data that the receiver lacks in chunks of at most
32 KiB, each preceded by its length. When rsync runs over hashsync ssh,
these lengths split the file data in the stream every 32 KiB, and a block
of the receiver's files that one of them splits doesn't match. Depending on
how the data is aligned, that can be up to block_size / 32 KiB of the
blocks, or half of them with hashsync's default 16 KiB blocks. With chunks
of up to 8 MiB, almost every block matches.

Only the rsync that sends the files needs the patch: the remote one in a
pull, the local one in a push. The patch is backwards compatible, so the
receiving rsync can be unpatched.

The sender now buffers up to 16 MiB of output, and reads up to 24 MiB of a
file ahead of what it has sent. With --bwlimit, it sends the buffered data
of the earlier files before it opens the next one, so that it doesn't read
a file long before it sends it (rsync's growing-file test checks this).

If a block of a file matches a block of the receiver's copy, rsync sends
only the block's index, in 4 bytes. If a file mostly matches, the sender's
output for the whole file can then fit in its buffer, and the receiver
would get nothing until the sender had read the whole file. So after each
matching block, the sender still sends its output once 64 KiB are
waiting, like unpatched rsync, and the receiver can copy the matching
blocks while the sender reads the rest of the file.

With --max-alloc, rsync exits if it has to allocate that many bytes or
more at once. When the patched rsync sends files, it allocates 16 MiB at
once for its output buffer, which holds two chunks of 8 MiB. So if
--max-alloc is less than 32 MiB, the patched rsync halves the chunk size
until the output buffer is at most half of --max-alloc. For example, with
--max-alloc=16M, the chunks are 4 MiB, and with --max-alloc=1M, the
smallest value that rsync accepts, they are 256 KiB. rsync's
daemon-delete-msg-overflow test uses --max-alloc=1M.

With --progress, the patched rsync also shows the progress before it sends
a chunk of more than 32 KiB, and not only after. rsync measures the
transfer rate of a file from the first progress update of the file, so
without this, rsync would show a rate of 0 for a file that it sends in one
chunk. rsync's output-options test checks the rate.

